1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
A local lawmaker is calling for a federal review of the North Oddblock project at Konell Side.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:11,000
Congressman Nick Langworthy is questioning how the project's public funding changed after the developer was selected.

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:14,000
Our Angelica Gallagher joins us with the very latest.

4
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:15,000
Angelica.

5
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:21,000
Marley, project construction is already underway on what state the state says will transform long-time blight in the city.

6
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:29,000
But Langworthy says for him, the way this project is being funded also raises questions about the competitive bidding process before a developer was even selected.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:36,000
I'm not accusing anyone of anything, but let's examine where all laws followed.

8
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:49,000
Is this project consistent with the spirit in the law as it comes to the low-income housing tax credit in the 30 million federal dollars that are being subsidized here?

9
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:52,000
The North Oddblock at Konell Side has sat vacant for decades.

10
00:00:53,000 --> 00:01:06,000
Now, Penrose, the Philadelphia-based developer chosen through a state process in 2023, is building a $225 million mixed-use development with 251 apartments, 187 of them affordable housing.

11
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:13,000
But Western New York Congressman Nick Langworthy says the project's public funding changed significantly after Penrose was selected.

12
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:23,000
What caught my attention was the vast amount of subsidy that's going into creating this housing on this very sought-after piece of property.

13
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:28,000
Langworthy says initially the project was advertised as having $10 million in public funding.

14
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:40,000
Now we're looking at a staggering sum of over $600,000 per housing unit that's going to be put on this that is going to come from either federal, state, or local taxpayers.

15
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:54,000
There's an excess of $30 million just in federal monies and an additional $50-plus million in state credits as well as a 30-year payment in lieu of taxes.

16
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:58,000
It's a staggering sum of public money.

17
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:01,000
Langworthy serves on the House Committee on Oversight and Government Reform.

18
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:07,000
He believes if advertised as is now, it would have drawn a local developer to use that local funding.

19
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:20,000
In a letter to the Office of the Inspector General, he asks the U.S. Department of Housing and Urban Development, or HUD, to review whether the changes raise questions about the competitiveness of the selection process and the use of federal housing resources.

20
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:36,000
The most important thing is to investigate whether or not the material change in the bid after it was awarded and those circumstances changing is consistent with federal law to make sure that everything was on the up-and-up.

21
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:38,000
And if it is, so be it.

